    "sectionId": "good-to-know",
    "heading": "Good to know",
    "text": "Georgia: registering with the Secretary of State, then renewing every year and amending after an officer change. Also the annual members' meeting with 21 days' notice, minutes and financial records, and a statement of amounts due within five business days. The last three come from the Property Owners' Association Act, which covers property submitted to it. If yours isn't, check your governing documents.\nGeorgia corporate annual registration: if the association is incorporated as a Georgia nonprofit corporation (most are), it files an annual registration with the Secretary of State between January 1 and April 1 every year. This is a different filing from the SB 406 registration. Each year has its own row, so marking 2027 done doesn't hide 2028. After April 1 the row turns red. More than 60 days late, the Secretary of State may start administratively dissolving the corporation. The row quotes §§ 14-3-1622 and 14-3-1420 and links the Secretary of State's filing page for the fee.\nGeorgia annual meeting: hold it before your fiscal year ends. If the board doesn't, owners holding 5% of the voting power (or your bylaws' figure, never more than 25%) may call it themselves.\nDirector elections (Georgia, Florida, California, Texas): one row for each election in the election planner. It shows the next date on the election's timeline, dates that break the rules, and whether the results are recorded. A date that has passed says to check it was done, because HomeownerHub can't tell.\nCalifornia: the row turns red if the results notice is more than 15 days late and not recorded as sent.\nGeorgia, after the results are recorded: a row to amend your Secretary of State registration within 30 days if the officers changed. HomeownerHub counts from the day the results were recorded; if the board picks officers later, count from that day. Before Jan 1, 2027 it says to list the new officers in your first registration. Check the Secretary of State's guidance.\nTexas: the management certificate. Florida: director education certificates (tracked on Members). California: the annual budget report window (from the budget's adoption checklist).\nThe monthly board report adds a Director elections line with the most pressing election item.\nGeorgia's condominium rules aren't loaded. The page says \"check your governing documents\" for those.\nThe page quotes statutes word for word. It isn't legal advice. Ask your association's attorney.",
